{"kind":"report","text":"Approved contract amendment 2 revision 2 at exact head a41f227dee317e905f93f2264d89ee87b9228c71. Primary: docs/protocol.md; all 11 submitted artifact bases are included. This approves the shared contract and its stated integration work, not completion of the Room, clients, verifier or deployed platform.\n\nReview 152f29e8 is answered. R-GEN-12 separates fresh public allocation from authorized import. Public callers cannot choose a pre-existing repository; an import carries an operator-signed grant for the repository identity and first admin key inside the signed genesis, checked again at found. R-GEN-10 orders authorization and binding before repository access, credentials and sealing. R-GEN-13 atomically binds repository identity, Room ID and convenience name, keeps incomplete founding bound, permits the identical retry to complete forward, and refuses any conflicting binding. R-PUB-10 ties canonical publication authority to that binding. Normative cases cover unauthorized import, edited genesis, isolated allocation, simultaneous founding, duplicate import, aliases and failure after binding followed by an expired-grant retry.\n\nThe two requested clarifications are also explicit: queued notify evaluation and replay use the policy active when the original entry was sealed; bearer authentication occurs before envelope construction, so revoked/expired bearers do not have the retained-signed-envelope replay exception. Their acceptance cases make the boundaries reviewable.\n\nArchitecture: one registry binding is a coherent source of repository identity, lookup and publication authority within the deployment. Required lane edits cover the new repository source, registry, bearer adapter, RPC byte decoding, attention snapshot, recomputation/readiness events and verifier grant checks. The integration list also names operator-key configuration, grant tooling, public-founding secret, reserved namespace and repository creation. Keep that complete scope when wiring the real Artifacts port. No independent deployment may be assumed to share this registry automatically: operating the same imported repository from multiple deployments needs an explicit shared ownership boundary or exclusive operator allocation; the unexpected-writer publication rules remain necessary.\n\nSecurity: the first admin signature now proves possession while the operator grant or isolated allocation establishes repository authority. The grant is covered by genesis and is specific to repository identity and admin key. The expiration exception is limited to the repository already bound to the identical Room ID, so it repairs incomplete founding without admitting a second Room. Verification checks the grant and reports the operator key; readers choose their trust, and log verification does not claim to prove the external registry or operator's ownership process. RepoIdentity is a string type; stable identity validation and alias refusal remain runtime requirements, and type checking alone is not evidence they are enforced.\n\nSimplification: deriving name lookup and publication authority from one binding avoids competing sources of identity. Public founding and import are explicit source variants. Keeping the existing ten MCP tools while fixing byte streams, because passthrough, same-page publication metadata and structured unknown-explain results is sound. The listed state-rederivation and succession limitations are explicit; they do not declare those broader capabilities complete or cut requested scope by schedule.\n\nValidation: clean installation and repository type checks passed. Policy tests passed 189 on Node and 188 in workerd, with its one documented Node-only compiler test skipped there. All 88 UI tests passed. An independent compile fixture accepts public/import source variants and the onboarding signing domain, and rejects caller-chosen string sources, imports without a grant and an existing repository named in a new source. Reviewed the changed protocol sections and all 11 artifact bases, including lifecycle examples and exports; unchanged amendment content retains the prior inspection. The only UI source difference from the approved UI v3 head is the added land-input-changed description, which compiles with the new retry union. No deployed Room, repository, token or service was touched.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:95e9d7a47ded41dbb825870349878579e341698e","binding":"assigned","head":"a41f227dee317e905f93f2264d89ee87b9228c71","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:81d60fbd444fdb1dc772e0082a3e9554a7655489\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bf17b0654beb0b883f5c4f9b22ba4aa1d2e692fd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:366e1a296e87e7eef5ef27f6e86943e9f868b407","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}